Background: I'm a freelance product designer who's started building small React prototypes myself instead of handing Figma files to devs. I made a filterable project gallery and it works, but a dev friend glanced at it and said "too many useStates" without explaining. Here's the component:

```jsx
function Gallery({ projects }) {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState('');
  const [category, setCategory] = useState('all');
  const [filtered, setFiltered] = useState(projects);
  const [count, setCount] = useState(projects.length);

  useEffect(() => {
    const result = projects.filter(p =>
      (category === 'all' || p.category === category) &&
      p.title.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase())
    );
    setFiltered(result);
    setCount(result.length);
  }, [search, category, projects]);

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={search} onChange={e => setSearch(e.target.value)} />
      <select value={category} onChange={e => setCategory(e.target.value)}>...</select>
      <p>{count} projects</p>
      {filtered.map(p => <Card key={p.id} project={p} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

What did they mean?

turns.assistant:
Your friend probably meant that `filtered` and `count` shouldn't be state at all. They're **derived** from `search`, `category` and `projects`, so you can calculate them during render.

Here's the simpler version:

```jsx
function Gallery({ projects }) {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState('');
  const [category, setCategory] = useState('all');

  const filtered = projects.filter(p =>
    (category === 'all' || p.category === category) &&
    p.title.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase())
  );

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={search} onChange={e => setSearch(e.target.value)} />
      <select value={category} onChange={e => setCategory(e.target.value)}>...</select>
      <p>{filtered.length} projects</p>
      {filtered.map(p => <Card key={p.id} project={p} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Why it's better:
- **Less code** and no `useEffect`.
- **No extra render:** your version renders once with old `filtered`, then the effect runs and triggers another render.
- **No risk of getting out of sync:** values that are calculated can't disagree with their source.

A good rule: if you can compute it from props or other state, don't store it in state.

